From 2d330158570fc9da6baa0b0664514edeafb15ba7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: exeroom536 <352890649+exeroom536@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 12:54:12 +0500
Subject: [PATCH 45/48] super-amd: add disks to a RAID5, and make a RAID1 of
 two a RAID5

mdadm --grow <RAID5> --raid-devices=N and <RAID1 of two> --level=5
--layout=right-asymmetric --raid-devices=N are recorded as the AMD
driver records the migration (rcadm -T -r5): the transform log, then
the volume's LD with NORMAL and the RAID5 it becomes, the members where
they are, the new ones on spares of the container, the data of each
moving from +0x5000 down to +0x800 of its extent.  md reshapes it moving
data_offset so - container_content() gives the new offsets, for
set_metadata_data_offset() (earlier in this series) - held behind the
transform log: manage_reshape_amd() writes the log before each region,
holds writes to the region until md has moved it and lets md on to its
end only (sync_max), so that the AMD driver - or md after a crash -
copying the region again from where the data were finds them there.
mdmon writes the "100 %" and the final packet as md ends.  The log, the
protect window and the transform flags of a migration are those of the
LD it makes (the flags of the one it starts from differ in where its own
data are).  A disk that does not take the log of a region is failed in
md if md reshapes on without it, as the AMD driver fails its element,
and the reshape goes on degraded; else the reshape waits at the region,
its writes held - as they are while md is not reshaping (frozen,
interrupted): let through, they would go where md has moved part of
the region, which is copied again from the log at its start, over them.

A volume midway through such a migration, ours or the AMD driver's, is
assembled reshaping from the mark of its log (the furthest copy) - from
0 if nothing has moved yet: md moves the data out of their own way, as
the AMD driver begins such a migration again.  Of several volumes the
AMD driver left midway in a container, each is assembled with its
reshape, and Grow.c goes on with one after another, md holding the
others where they are meanwhile.

mdadm starts such a migration as the AMD driver checks one: a whole
volume in sync, no other transform, 8 disks at most as -C (--force:
more); one in a container at a time; every member's disk with the live
configuration (one that joined later would keep its data where they are
while md moves the others'); and not for data an earlier migration
moved already (AMD moves them on with a log of the data, which mdadm
does not write).  No backup file: nothing is backed up.

Tested on an AMD X870E board (SATA HDDs and SSDs): a RAID5 of 3 disks
given a fourth and a RAID1 of two made a RAID5 of three - md check
clean, the data the same, the BIOS menu shows them Normal with the new
capacity, and the AMD Windows driver reads the same data; one of ours
stopped midway, the Windows driver finished itself, and md ran it after.
With the AMD Linux driver (rcraid 9.3.3) in a QEMU guest: either goes on
with the other's migration stopped midway, rcraid with ours stopped
before its first region too, the data the same.

Written by Claude Opus 5.5, an AI model by Anthropic.
